# Hashish — a pressed slab of sifted resin.
#
# PLACEHOLDER art. Warlon Mhite may redraw it; that is what this file is for.
#
# ## Translation: what makes it hash and not a cookie or a mud pie
#
# The first draft of this was an octagonal blob and read as neither. The fix was the archetype, not
# the palette: **vanilla never draws a block-shaped item as an axis-aligned rectangle, and never as
# a circle either — it draws an oblique slab**, a flat plate tilted so you see the top face and the
# near edge at once. The silhouette here is vanilla's own `item/brick`, measured out of the client
# jar, because a pressed lump of hash is the same object: a small dense slab somebody made in a
# mould or a press.
#
# What separates it from a brick is entirely colour and surface. Hash is much darker and browner
# than the blond kief it is pressed from, the ramp runs to near-black at the rim, and the highlight
# sits in one cluster up-left rather than as a gradient across the whole sprite (a straight
# corner-to-corner fade explains only 9-16% of the brightness in real vanilla items — that is
# pancake shading, and it is the classic tell).
#
# Items are high contrast: vanilla items span a median 164 luminance steps against a block's 76.
# So this looks nothing like block/sifting_box_pressed even though it is the same material, and that
# is correct rather than an inconsistency.

@palette default
. = none
1 = 130D05      deepest shadow, and the rim on the unlit side
2 = 251A0A      rim
3 = 3A2911
4 = 4E3818
5 = 664A21
6 = 7E5D2B      the body of the top face
7 = 9A7439      pressed sheen; one cluster, up-left, and nowhere else

@texture item/hashish 16x16 type=item
